\[\nn = |V(T)| = ds + 1 + ds(ds + 1) = (ds + 1)^2,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_d^2(T) = 1 + ds^2,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_d^2(T) = 1 + ds^2 = \\frac{(ds + 1)^2 - 2(ds + 1) + d + 1}{d} = \\frac{n - 2\\sqrt{n} + d + 1}{d}.\n\\]\n\nInspired by the result from [9] asserting that \\(\\gamma_d^1(G) \\le \\frac{n-2\\sqrt{n}+d+1}{d}\\), and by Theorem 1.5, we propose the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 6.2. If \\(3 \\le p \\le d\\) and \\(T\\) is a tree of order \\(n\\), then \\(\\gamma_d^p(T) \\le \\frac{n-2\\sqrt{n}+d+1}{d}\\).\n\nNote that if Conjecture 6.2 is true, then the above example can be used to show that the bound is sharp.\n\n7. Spanning trees\n\nLet \\(e\\) be an edge of a graph \\(G\\). Since \\(d_{G-e}(u,v) \\ge d(u,v)\\) for every \\(u,v \\in V(G)\\), a packing of \\(G\\) remains a packing in \\(G-e\\). On the other hand, this property in general does not extend to \\(\\gamma_2^2(G)\\). To see it, consider the graphs \\(G_p\\) and \\(G'_p\\) (\\(p \\ge 2\\)) as shown in Fig. 4, where \\(\\deg(u_i)=\\deg(v_i)=p+2\\) for \\(i \\in [2]\\).\n\nNote that \\(\\mathrm{ecc}_{G_p}(u_1)=2\\), hence \\(\\gamma_2^2(G_p)=1\\). Since \\(\\gamma_2^2(G_p-e_1)=1+p\\), this example shows that \\(\\gamma_2^2\\) can increase arbitrary by removing an edge. On the other hand, \\(\\{u_2,v_2\\}\\) is a \\(\\gamma_2^2\\)-set of \\(G'_p-e\\), so \\(\\gamma_2^2(G'_p-e_2)=2\\), but \\(\\gamma_2^2(G'_p)=1+p\\), hence \\(\\gamma_2^2\\) can also decrease arbitrary by removing an edge.\n\nSpringer--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevisiting d-distance (independent) domination in trees and in bipartite graphs (https://dirros.openscience.si/Dokument.php?id=37411&lang=eng)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 10 months ago; The *d*-distance *p*-packing domination number γᵈₚ(G) of G is the minimum size of a set of vertices of G which is both a *d*-distance dominating set and a *p*-packing. ... In 1994, Beineke and Henning conjectured that if d ≥ 1 and T is a tree of order n ≥ d + 1, then γᵈ₁(T) ≤ n/(d+1). They supported the conjecture by proving it for d ∈ {1, 2, 3}.\nDiscrete Mathematics 349 (2026) 114972\n\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect  \nDiscrete Mathematics  \njournal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/disc\n\nRevisiting d-distance (independent) domination in trees and in bipartite graphs\n\nCsilla Bujtásᵃ,ᵇ, Vesna Iršič Chenowethᵃ,ᵇ, Sandi Klavžarᵃ,ᵇ,ᶜ,*, Gang Zhangᵇ,ᵈ\n\nᵃ Faculty of Mathematics and Physics, University of Ljubljana, Slovenia  \nᵇ Institute of Mathematics, Physics and Mechanics, Ljubljana, Slovenia  \nᶜ Faculty of Natural Sciences and Mathematics, University of Maribor, Slovenia  \nᵈ School of Mathematical Sciences, Xiamen University, China\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nSmall “Check for updates” badge/icon at the right of the title area.\n</visual_element>\n\n<table id=\"e2\">\n<tr>\n<td>\n\n**ARTICLE INFO**\n\n**Article history:**  \nReceived 18 August 2025  \nAccepted 28 December 2025  \nAvailable online xxxx\n\n**Keywords:**  \nd-distance dominating set  \np-packing set  \nTree  \nBipartite graph\n\n</td>\n<td>\n\n**ABSTRACT**\n\nThe *d*-distance *p*-packing domination number γᵈₚ(G) of G is the minimum size of a set of vertices of G which is both a *d*-distance dominating set and a *p*-packing. Then S is a *d*-distance dominating set of G if every vertex u ∈ V(G) \\ S there exists a vertex w ∈ S such that d(u, w) ≤ d, and S is a *p*-packing of G if d(w, w′) ≥ p + 1 for every two different vertices w, w′ ∈ S. The *d*-distance *p*-packing domination number γᵈₚ(G) of G is the minimum size of a set S which is at the same time a *d*-distance dominating set and *p*-packing. (If for some parameters d and p such a set does not exist, set γᵈₚ(G) = ∞.)\n\nThe *d*-distance *p*-packing domination number was introduced by Beineke and Henning [1] under the name *(p, d)*-domination number and with the notation iₚ,ᵈ(G). With the intention of placing it within the trends of contemporary graph domination theory, the notation γᵈₚ(G) was recently proposed in [2] and we follow it here. In [2] it is proved that for every two fixed integers d and p with 2 ≤ d and 0 ≤ p ≤ 2d − 1, the decision problem whether γᵈₚ(G) ≤ k holds is NP-complete for bipartite planar graphs. Several bounds on γᵈₚ(T), where T is a tree on n vertices with ℓ leaves and s support vertices are also proved, including γ²₀(T) ≥ (n−ℓ−s+4)/5 and γᵈ₂(T) ≤ (n−2√(n+d+1))/d, d ≥ 2. These results improve or extend earlier results from the literature.\n\n* Corresponding author.  \nE-mail addresses: csilla.bujtas@fmf.uni-lj.si (C. Bujtás), vesna.irsic@fmf.uni-lj.si (V. Iršič Chenoweth), sandi.klavzar@fmf.uni-lj.si (S. Klavžar), gzhang@stu.xmu.edu.cn (G. Zhang).\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2025.114972  \n0012-365X/© 2025 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevisiting $d$-distance (independent) domination in trees and in bipartite graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.12804)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; The $d$-distance $p$-packing domination number $\\gamma_d^p(G)$ of $G$ is the minimum size of a set of vertices of $G$ which is both a $d$-distance dominating set and a $p$-packing. In 1994, Beineke and Henning conjectured that if $d\\ge 1$ and $T$ is a tree of order $n \\geq d+1$, then $\\gamma_d^1(T) \\leq \\frac{n}{d+1}$. They supported the conjecture by proving it for $d\\in \\{1,2,3\\}$.\nTitle: Revisiting $d$-distance (independent) domination in trees and in bipartite graphs\nAuthors: Csilla Bujtás, Vesna Iršič Chenoweth, Sandi Klavžar, Gang Zhang\nDate: Mon Aug 18 10:38:10 2025\n\nThe $d$-distance $p$-packing domination number $\\gamma_d^p(G)$ of $G$ is the minimum size of a set of vertices of $G$ which is both a $d$-distance dominating set and a $p$-packing. In 1994, Beineke and Henning conjectured that if $d\\ge 1$ and $T$ is a tree of order $n \\geq d+1$, then $\\gamma_d^1(T) \\leq \\frac{n}{d+1}$. They supported the conjecture by proving it for $d\\in \\{1,2,3\\}$. In this paper, it is proved that $\\gamma_d^1(G) \\leq \\frac{n}{d+1}$ holds for any bipartite graph $G$ of order $n \\geq d+1$, and any $d\\ge 1$. Trees $T$ for which $\\gamma_d^1(T) = \\frac{n}{d+1}$ holds are characterized. It is also proved that if $T$ has $\\ell$ leaves, then $\\gamma_d^1(T) \\leq \\frac{n-\\ell}{d}$ (provided that $n-\\ell \\geq d$), and $\\gamma_d^1(T) \\leq \\frac{n+\\ell}{d+2}$ (provided that $n\\geq d$). The latter result extends Favaron's theorem from 1992 asserting that $\\gamma_1^1(T) \\leq \\frac{n+\\ell}{3}$. In both cases, trees that attain the equality are characterized and relevant conclusions for the $d$-distance domination number of trees derived.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNeural ideals and stimulus space visualization (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0196885817301434)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; We then show that the toric ideal of a 1-inductively pierced code is generated by quadratics and give preliminary evidence for a stronger conjecture regarding 1-inductively pierced codes. ... The statement holds for , in this case, there is a single code that is 1-inductively pierced, but not 0-inductively pierced; the toric ideal of {\\mathcal{C}}_{2} is generated by a single quadratic, . ... A well-formed neural code \\mathcal{C} on 3 neurons is 1-inductively pierced if and only if the Gröbner basis of {I}_{\\mathcal{C}} with respect to the weighted graded reverse lexicographic order with the weight vector contains only binomials of degree 2 or less.\nTherefore, is in the toric ideal {I}_{\\mathcal{C}}. If v is the all zeros codeword then . □\n\nIn essence, Propositio